 questions: [
  ["A 7-year-old has a deep occlusal lesion on 8.5 with brief pain to cold that stops when the stimulus is removed. Radiographs show no periradicular pathosis, and the lesion appears to approach the pulp. The most appropriate treatment is:","Indirect pulp treatment",["Pulpotomy","Pulpectomy","Extraction"],"Reversible pulpitis with deep caries is treated by leaving the deepest affected dentine and sealing it, which avoids an exposure. Pulpotomy is for an actual exposure."],
  ["Which of the following findings in a primary molar indicates irreversible pulpitis or necrosis rather than reversible pulpitis?","Spontaneous pain that wakes the child at night",["Pain while eating sweets that stops within seconds","Sensitivity to cold relieved by brushing","Pain only when food packs into the cavity"],"Spontaneous, unprovoked pain is a sign of irreversible pulpitis. Provoked pain of short duration relieved by removing the stimulus is reversible."],
  ["A radiolucency in the furcation of a primary mandibular molar, rather than at the root apices, is best explained by:","Accessory canals crossing the thin pulp floor",["Normal resorption by the erupting premolar","Periodontal disease from poor oral hygiene","Superimposition of the developing premolar crown"],"Pulp infection drains through accessory canals in the porous primary pulp floor, so the first radiolucency appears in the furcation. The developing premolar crown has its own follicle outline and is not a pathological radiolucency."],
  ["During a pulpotomy on 8.4, bleeding from the radicular stumps remains profuse and dark after five minutes of pressure with a moist cotton pellet. The most appropriate next step is:","Pulpectomy or extraction",["Place formocresol and proceed with the pulpotomy","Apply calcium hydroxide and restore","Place a direct pulp cap"],"Uncontrollable haemorrhage shows the radicular pulp is inflamed, so a pulpotomy would fail. The canals must be cleaned and filled, or the tooth removed."],
  ["The root canals of a primary molar treated by pulpectomy should be filled with:","Resorbable zinc oxide eugenol paste",["Gutta-percha with a resin sealer","MTA packed to the apex","Silver points"],"The filling must resorb with the primary root so the premolar can erupt. Gutta-percha does not resorb and may deflect the successor."],
  ["The AAPD recommends against which material as the pulpotomy medicament in primary teeth?","Calcium hydroxide",["Mineral trioxide aggregate","Formocresol","Ferric sulfate"],"Calcium hydroxide pulpotomies in primary teeth have lower success, often from internal resorption, and the AAPD's 2017 guideline recommended against them. Calcium silicates such as MTA now show the highest success."],
  ["Ferric sulfate used as a pulpotomy agent works mainly by:","Agglutinating blood proteins to seal the pulp stumps",["Fixing the radicular pulp tissue","Inducing a calcified dentine bridge","Sterilising the root canals with free radicals"],"Ferric sulfate is a haemostatic; the iron-protein complex forms a mechanical seal over the vessels. Tissue fixation is the action of formocresol."],
  ["An 8-year-old fractured 1.1 two days ago, exposing 2 mm of pulp. The apex is open and the tooth has no other injury. The most appropriate treatment is:","Partial (Cvek) pulpotomy with calcium hydroxide or MTA",["Pulpectomy and gutta-percha obturation","Extraction and a removable partial denture","Apexification with an MTA apical barrier"],"A vital, traumatically exposed immature tooth keeps its pulp so the root can mature, and inflammation after a few days is superficial. Apexification is for a non-vital tooth."],
  ["A 9-year-old has a necrotic 2.1 with an open apex and a periapical radiolucency. Among the following, the most appropriate treatment is:","Apexification with an MTA apical barrier",["Partial pulpotomy","Conventional root canal treatment with lateral condensation","Indirect pulp treatment"],"A dead pulp cannot continue root formation, so a barrier is created at the open apex before obturation; regenerative endodontics is the other option. Lateral condensation cannot seal a wide, open apex."],
  ["Apexogenesis is best defined as:","Continued physiological root development in a tooth with vital pulp",["Formation of a calcified barrier at the apex of a non-vital tooth","Replacement of necrotic pulp with a stem-cell scaffold","Closure of the apex by cementum after root canal treatment"],"Apexogenesis depends on keeping the pulp alive so it can lengthen and thicken the root. A barrier in a non-vital tooth is apexification."],
  ["Why is indirect pulp treatment successful even though some carious dentine is left under the restoration?","A complete seal deprives the remaining bacteria of substrate so the lesion arrests",["The liner dissolves the remaining caries","The pulp phagocytoses the residual bacteria","Fluoride from the restoration sterilises the dentine instantly"],"Bacteria sealed off from dietary sugar die or become inactive, and tertiary dentine forms beneath. A leaking restoration would let the lesion progress, which is why the seal is critical."],
  ["After a pulpotomy on a primary second molar with caries on three surfaces, the most appropriate restoration is:","A stainless steel crown",["A glass ionomer restoration","An amalgam restoration","A resin sealant"],"The AAPD indicates stainless steel crowns after pulpotomy or pulpectomy and for large multisurface lesions, and pulp-treated primary teeth have the best reported outcomes under them. A three-surface glass ionomer or amalgam restoration is far more likely to fail."],
  ["A direct pulp cap on a primary molar is most appropriate for:","A pinpoint mechanical exposure in a tooth with a normal pulp",["A carious exposure in a tooth with spontaneous pain","A large carious exposure with heavy bleeding","A tooth with a furcation radiolucency"],"Direct capping in primary teeth is limited to small non-carious exposures, where the pulp is uninfected. Carious exposures already contain bacteria, and spontaneous pain or furcation pathosis rules out vital therapy."]
 ]
