 questions: [
  ["A 4-year-old has caries on the mesial, occlusal and distal surfaces of 5.4 and is at high caries risk. The most durable restoration is:","A stainless steel crown",["A conventional glass ionomer restoration","A resin sealant","An amalgam Class I restoration"],"SSCs outlast intracoronal restorations in primary molars and are indicated for high-risk children with multisurface lesions. Conventional glass ionomer fails in multisurface cavities."],
  ["The gingival margin of a Class II preparation in a primary molar is not bevelled because:","Cervical enamel rods slope occlusally",["Primary enamel is thicker at the cervix","Amalgam needs a butt joint at every margin","The contact area is narrow and convex"],"In the cervical third of primary teeth the rods incline occlusally, so a flat gingival floor already supports the rod ends. The contact areas are broad and flat, not narrow."],
  ["While preparing a mesial box on 8.4, the pulp horn most likely to be exposed is the:","Mesiobuccal pulp horn",["Distobuccal pulp horn","Distolingual pulp horn","Distal pulp horn"],"The mesial horn of the mandibular first primary molar is the highest and closest to the surface. Distal horns are lower and away from a mesial box."],
  ["In the Hall technique, which of the following is performed?","Cementing a glass-ionomer-filled stainless steel crown without tooth preparation",["Complete caries removal under local anaesthesia","Occlusal reduction of 1.5 mm and proximal slices","Pulpotomy before crown placement"],"The Hall technique omits anaesthesia, caries removal and preparation and seals the lesion under the crown. Conventional SSC placement uses occlusal reduction and proximal slices."],
  ["The Hall technique controls caries in a primary molar mainly by:","Sealing the biofilm off from dietary substrate so the lesion arrests",["Removing all infected dentine mechanically","Releasing enough fluoride to remineralise the dentine fully","Stimulating the pulp to resorb the carious dentine"],"Bacteria sealed under a crown are starved of sugar and the lesion stops progressing, the same principle as indirect pulp treatment. Nothing is removed mechanically."],
  ["The Hall technique is contraindicated for a primary molar with:","A sinus tract and a furcation radiolucency",["A cavitated proximal lesion into dentine","An anxious child who refuses local anaesthesia","A lesion that has not yet been restored"],"A sinus and furcation radiolucency mean pulp necrosis; sealing over an infected pulp leaves an abscess. The other situations are typical Hall cases."],
  ["Why are orthodontic separators placed a few days before a Hall crown?","To open the proximal contacts so the crown can seat without preparation",["To anaesthetise the gingiva by pressure","To check whether the tooth is vital","To retract the gingiva for the impression"],"Without proximal slices the contacts are too tight for the crown; separators create the space. They have no anaesthetic or diagnostic role."],
  ["A 3-year-old needs full coverage of a decayed 5.1. The incisal half of the crown remains and the gingiva bleeds easily. Which is the main risk of choosing a resin strip crown?","Bleeding and moisture can prevent the composite from bonding",["It needs more tooth reduction than a zirconia crown","It cannot be shade-matched","It must be crimped to the cervical bulge"],"Strip crowns rely on bonding, so contamination by blood or saliva compromises retention and shade. Zirconia, not strip crowns, needs the greatest reduction."],
  ["Compared with a preveneered stainless steel crown, a preformed zirconia crown for a primary incisor:","Requires more tooth reduction because it cannot be crimped or adjusted",["Requires less tooth reduction","Is more likely to lose its facing","Can be crimped to improve fit"],"Zirconia crowns must seat passively, so the tooth is reduced 1.5-2 mm with a feather margin. Facing loss is the weakness of preveneered SSCs."],
  ["A 6-year-old at high caries risk has a partly erupted 3.6 with deep fissures that cannot be kept dry. The most appropriate preventive measure is:","A glass ionomer sealant as an interim measure",["A resin-based sealant placed without isolation","An occlusal amalgam restoration","Waiting until full eruption before any measure"],"Glass ionomer tolerates moisture better and releases fluoride, so it protects the fissures until a resin sealant can be placed under good isolation. Resin sealants fail when contaminated."],
  ["A 5-year-old has eight open carious lesions and treatment under general anaesthesia is scheduled in four months. The most appropriate interim measure is:","Interim therapeutic restorations with high-viscosity glass ionomer",["Amalgam restorations at the first visit without anaesthesia","No treatment until the general anaesthetic","Resin sealants over the cavities"],"ITRs reduce bacterial load and stop progression until definitive care, as the AAPD advises for multiple open lesions. Sealants cannot seal cavitated lesions."],
  ["Which of the following best explains why restorations placed in children under general anaesthesia are usually stainless steel crowns rather than multisurface fillings?","A failed restoration may mean a second general anaesthetic, so the most durable option is chosen",["Fillings cannot be placed under general anaesthesia","SSCs need no isolation","Children under general anaesthesia are always low caries risk"],"Children treated under GA are typically high risk, and retreatment carries the risk and cost of another anaesthetic, so durability decides. Fillings can be placed under GA but fail more often."]
 ]
