 questions: [
  ["A newborn has a mobile tooth in the position of 7.1 that is causing an ulcer on the underside of the tongue. The ulcer is best termed:","Riga-Fede disease",["Bohn nodule","Epstein pearl","Eruption haematoma"],"Riga-Fede disease is traumatic ulceration of the ventral tongue from a natal or neonatal incisor. Bohn nodules and Epstein pearls are keratin cysts of the alveolus and palate."],
  ["Most natal teeth are:","Prematurely erupted primary teeth",["Supernumerary teeth","Permanent teeth","Odontomas"],"Most natal teeth are the normal primary teeth erupting early, so extraction leaves a gap until the permanent tooth. Only a minority are supernumeraries."],
  ["A natal tooth in a 3-day-old is so mobile that it moves 2 mm in every direction. The main reason to extract it is:","Risk of aspiration",["Risk of dental caries","Interference with speech development","Risk of ankylosis"],"A hypermobile natal tooth with little root could detach and be inhaled. Speech is not a concern in a newborn."],
  ["A 7-year-old's bitewing shows 2.6 trapped beneath the distal bulge of 6.5, with resorption of the distal root of 6.5. The most likely diagnosis is:","Ectopic eruption of 2.6",["Ankylosis of 6.5","Primary failure of eruption of 2.6","Normal exfoliation of 6.5"],"A permanent first molar on a mesial path impacts on the primary second molar and resorbs its distal root. Ankylosis would show an infraoccluded primary molar with a lost PDL space."],
  ["Which of the following findings best distinguishes an ankylosed primary molar from a normally exfoliating one?","No mobility and a solid, high-pitched percussion note",["Root resorption seen on the radiograph","Presence of a developing successor","Location in the mandibular arch"],"Fusion to bone removes physiological mobility and gives a solid sound. Root resorption and a successor are present in normal exfoliation too."],
  ["An ankylosed primary molar appears to sink below the occlusal plane mainly because:","The alveolar process grows vertically while the fused tooth cannot follow",["The tooth is being intruded by occlusal forces","The root is resorbed faster than normal","The successor pulls the tooth downward"],"An ankylosed tooth is fixed in bone while neighbouring teeth erupt and the alveolus grows, so its relative height falls. It is not actively moving down."],
  ["A 12-year-old has an infraoccluded 7.5 and radiographs show that 3.5 is congenitally missing. The roots of 7.5 are intact. The most appropriate management is to:","Retain 7.5 and build up its occlusal surface",["Extract 7.5 immediately","Extract 7.5 and place a band-and-loop space maintainer","Apply orthodontic force to extrude 7.5"],"Without a successor the primary molar can serve for years, so keeping it and restoring its height prevents tipping and overeruption. Orthodontic extrusion does not work on an ankylosed tooth."],
  ["A 9-year-old has a single brown, pitted enamel defect on 4.5. Records show that 8.5 had a chronic periapical abscess at age 5. The most likely diagnosis is:","Turner's tooth (local hypoplasia from the predecessor's infection)",["Amelogenesis imperfecta","Dental fluorosis","Molar-incisor hypomineralisation"],"Infection around the primary molar's roots disturbed the ameloblasts of the developing premolar crown. Amelogenesis imperfecta and fluorosis affect many teeth symmetrically."],
  ["A tooth appears to be a double crown. When it is counted as one tooth, the patient has one fewer tooth than normal in that arch. The most likely anomaly is:","Fusion",["Gemination","Concrescence","Dilaceration"],"Fusion joins two germs, so one fewer tooth is counted. In gemination one germ splits and the count stays normal."],
  ["Which of the following is the most common supernumerary tooth?","Mesiodens",["Paramolar of the maxilla","Distomolar of the mandible","Supernumerary mandibular premolar"],"The mesiodens, between the maxillary central incisors, is the most frequent supernumerary and a common cause of delayed central incisor eruption. Many supernumeraries suggest cleidocranial dysplasia."],
  ["A newly erupted 1.2 has a deep palatal pit and a radiograph shows an enamel-lined infolding. The most appropriate preventive step is to:","Seal the pit soon after eruption",["Extract the tooth","Perform a prophylactic pulpectomy","Review in five years"],"Dens invaginatus lets bacteria reach the pulp through the invagination, so early sealing prevents pulp necrosis in an immature tooth. Extraction and pulpectomy treat problems that have not occurred."],
  ["A 3-year-old has lost several mandibular primary incisors without trauma, with intact roots and no inflammation. Analysis shows absent cementum. The most likely diagnosis is:","Hypophosphatasia",["Cleidocranial dysplasia","Hypothyroidism","Ectodermal dysplasia"],"Low alkaline phosphatase prevents cementum formation, so primary teeth lose their attachment and exfoliate early with intact roots. Cleidocranial dysplasia and hypothyroidism delay rather than hasten tooth loss."]
 ]
